---
// Migrate from a GitHub wiki: written from an end-to-end run of the
// blume-migrate skill (SKILL.md and references/github-wiki.md) on a real
// wiki: the microsoft/TypeScript wiki (CC BY 4.0), cloned from
// github.com/microsoft/TypeScript.wiki.git at
// 966988bcca7c835fd22ab066bb6a9ff4d5ba511d (78 page files and 77 served URLs,
// since one README is shadowed by another; a _Sidebar.md with 6 groups over
// 26 pages, 50 pages it doesn't list, a _Footer.md, 45 wiki links (13 in
// served pages, 26 in the sidebar, 6 on the shadowed README), images stored
// in the wiki, pages in subfolders, and a two-way sync with a mirror
// repository), with Blume 2.1.3 (main). The agent read only the skill and the
// Blume docs it points to, converted a copy of the wiki clone in place, and
// wrote one-off scripts for the mechanical part. Outcome: `blume build`
// passed with 61 pages and `blume validate --strict` passed (one
// BLUME_ASSETS_UNCHECKED warning). All 77 old URLs were accounted for in the
// route table: 62 to built pages (one of them to an anchor), 9 to the
// external targets of moved notices, 6 left out on purpose (the mirror's
// README and SECURITY files, a test page, a script fixture, two empty
// deprecated pages), 0 misses. 71 link stubs were prepared locally and never
// pushed; the inbound links in microsoft/TypeScript were listed, not edited.
// After the run: the bundled codemod (scripts/github-wiki-codemod.mjs),
// written from the run's script, was applied to a fresh copy with the run's
// classification. It made the same mechanical edits (13 wiki links, 23 wiki
// URLs, 4 bare URLs, the mirror link, 7 alerts, 26 fence languages, 7 named
// anchors, heading demotion on 31 pages, 13 image references) and deleted 53
// shadowed link definitions (the run deleted 49; the other 4 were https
// duplicates CommonMark never used either). Its review list matched the
// run's hand fixes; `blume build` passed; `validate --strict` reported only
// those hand-fix items (5 glossary links, 2 missing pages, 3 anchors, and a
// false positive on a fence inside a blockquote); a second pass changed
// nothing; its `check` found 0 problems; and its `stubs` wrote the same 71
// stubs. It also ran on seven other public wikis (microsoft/vscode, koreader,
// MultiMC, qBittorrent, ruTorrent, gollum, Waybar) without errors, and six
// of them built with no hand edits (not validated). The vscode result failed
// to build on one page's `<details>` blocks inside list items, which
// `convert` reports.
// Review (after the above): the codemod was fixed for a second `convert`
// that rewrote the meta.ts files when a page's folder shared its name, wiki
// links inside HTML blocks (now `<a href>`) and `<code>`, image tags with
// comma options or caption text, and raw `dir/Page.md` links. The TypeScript
// copy then converted byte-identically to before, and `routes` plus
// `convert` ran on all 23 surveyed public wikis with no errors, a second
// `convert` changing nothing, and every fenced and inline code sample
// unchanged apart from alert bodies losing their `> ` and dropped broken
// images (not built). `check` over the 77 old URLs read from the saved
// `_pages` HTML gave 62 ok, 9 moved, and 6 DROPPED, matching the run.
// The Acme example is invented: a seven-page wiki, run through `routes`,
// `convert`, `blume build`, `blume validate --strict`, `check`, and `stubs`
// as shown. Every output below is real.
// Not run: `npx blume migrate github-wiki` itself (2.1.3 has no github-wiki
// source; the agent was pointed at the skill), pushing stubs to any wiki,
// disabling a wiki, a deploy, or a lockfile regeneration. What a disabled
// wiki's old URLs return is unverified, so this page doesn't say.
import Callout from "blume/components/content/Callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "blume/components/content/CodeBlock.astro";
import GuidePage from "../_guides/GuidePage.astro";
import { guideById } from "../_guides/guides.ts";

const codemod = "node_modules/blume/skills/blume-migrate/scripts/github-wiki-codemod.mjs";

const cloneWiki = [
  "git clone https://github.com/acme/widget.wiki.git",
  "cp -R widget.wiki widget-docs",
  "cd widget-docs",
  "git remote remove origin",
].join("\n");

const listPages = [
  "curl -s https://github.com/acme/widget/wiki/_pages \\",
  "  | grep -oE 'href=\"/acme/widget/wiki(/[^\"]*)?\"' \\",
  "  | sed -e 's/^href=\"//' -e 's/\"$//' -e \"s/&#39;/'/g\" \\",
  "  | grep -vE '/wiki/_(new|pages|history|compare)$' | sort -u > ../old-pages.txt",
].join("\n");

const beforePage = [
  "# Getting started",
  "",
  "Install the CLI, then read [[Configuration]] or [[the FAQ|FAQ#install-errors]].",
  "",
  "> [!WARNING]",
  "> Widget 1.x configs don't load in 2.0.",
  "",
  "![Dashboard](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/wiki/acme/widget/images/dashboard.png)",
  "",
  "# Next steps",
  "",
  "See [Plugins](https://github.com/acme/widget/wiki/Plugins).",
].join("\n");

const afterPage = [
  "---",
  "title: Getting Started",
  "---",
  "",
  "Install the CLI, then read [Configuration](/configuration) or [the FAQ](/faq#install-errors).",
  "",
  ":::warning",
  "Widget 1.x configs don't load in 2.0.",
  ":::",
  "",
  "![Dashboard](../images/dashboard.png)",
  "",
  "## Next steps",
  "",
  "See [Plugins](/plugins).",
].join("\n");

const sidebar = [
  "**Start**",
  "* [[Getting Started]]",
  "* [[Configuration]]",
  "",
  "**Reference**",
  "* [[FAQ]]",
  "* [[Plugins]]",
  "* [Changelog](https://github.com/acme/widget/releases)",
].join("\n");

const tree = [
  "docs/",
  "├── index.md                 ← Home.md",
  "├── meta.ts                  start, reference, more",
  "├── (start)/",
  "│   ├── meta.ts",
  "│   ├── getting-started.mdx",
  "│   └── configuration.md",
  "├── (reference)/",
  "│   ├── meta.ts",
  "│   ├── faq.md",
  "│   └── plugins.md",
  "├── (more)/",
  "│   ├── meta.ts              \"More pages\", collapsed",
  "│   └── release-process.md",
  "└── images/",
  "    └── dashboard.png",
].join("\n");

const startMeta = [
  'import { defineMeta } from "blume";',
  "",
  "export default defineMeta({",
  '  title: "Start",',
  "  pages: [",
  '    "getting-started",',
  '    "configuration",',
  "  ],",
  "});",
].join("\n");

const routesOutput = [
  "7 page(s)",
  "",
  "Looks like a moved notice: set its entry to the target",
  '  "Old-Setup": "/getting-started"',
].join("\n");

const routeTable = [
  "{",
  '  "Configuration": "/configuration",',
  '  "FAQ": "/faq",',
  '  "Getting-Started": "/getting-started",',
  '  "Home": "/",',
  '  "Old-Setup": "/getting-started",',
  '  "Plugins": "/plugins",',
  '  "Release-Process": "/release-process"',
  "}",
].join("\n");

const pushStubs = [
  "git clone https://github.com/acme/widget.wiki.git widget-stubs",
  "cd widget-stubs",
  "cp ../widget-docs/wiki-routes.json ../widget-docs/wiki-files.json .",
  `node ../widget-docs/${codemod} \\`,
  "  stubs --site https://docs.acme.dev --write",
  "rm wiki-routes.json wiki-files.json",
  "git add -A",
  'git commit -m "Point every page at the new docs"',
  "git push",
].join("\n");

const stubPage = "**This page has moved to <https://docs.acme.dev/getting-started>.**";

const checkInbound = [
  `node ${codemod} check \\`,
  "  'https://github.com/acme/widget/wiki/FAQ#install-errors' \\",
  "  'https://github.com/acme/widget/wiki/Getting-Started#setup' \\",
  "  'https://github.com/acme/widget/wiki/Old-Setup'",
].join("\n");

const checkInboundOutput = [
  "ok  https://github.com/acme/widget/wiki/FAQ#install-errors → /faq#install-errors",
  "MISSING ANCHOR  https://github.com/acme/widget/wiki/Getting-Started#setup → /getting-started#setup",
  "ok  https://github.com/acme/widget/wiki/Old-Setup → /getting-started",
  "",
  "1 problem(s).",
].join("\n");

const buildAndCheck = ["npm run build", `node ${codemod} check`].join("\n");

const checkOldPages = `node ${codemod} check $(sed 's#^#https://github.com#' ../old-pages.txt)`;

const checkTable = [
  "ok  Configuration → /configuration",
  "ok  FAQ → /faq",
  "ok  Getting-Started → /getting-started",
  "ok  Home → /",
  "ok  Old-Setup → /getting-started",
  "ok  Plugins → /plugins",
  "ok  Release-Process → /release-process",
  "",
  "0 problem(s).",
].join("\n");

const quotedFence = [
  "> Why is this an error?",
  ">",
  "> ```ts",
  "> interface StringMap {",
  ">   [key: string]: string;",
  "> }",
  "> ```",
].join("\n");
---

<GuidePage guide={guideById("migrate-from-github-wiki")}>
  <p>
    By the end of this guide, your GitHub wiki is a Blume site: every page at a
    clean URL, your <code>_Sidebar.md</code> rebuilt as the sidebar, wiki links
    and GitHub alerts converted, the wiki's images moved beside the pages, and
    a link stub ready for every old wiki page. A coding agent does the
    conversion with a codemod that ships with Blume, and you review it.
  </p>
  <p>
    It's written for wikis in Markdown. Pages in the other formats GitHub
    renders, like MediaWiki and AsciiDoc, are converted to Markdown with
    pandoc first.
  </p>

  <h2>What carries over</h2>
  <p>
    The run behind this guide migrated a real wiki: 77 pages, a sidebar with
    six groups, pages in subfolders, images stored in the wiki, and a sync
    with a mirror repository. Every content page carried over, and every old
    URL is accounted for: a new page, the page a moved notice pointed to, or a
    page left out on purpose and reported. Here's how the pieces map:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">In the wiki</th>
        <th scope="col">In Blume</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>Home.md</code></td>
        <td><code>docs/index.md</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Every other page, in any folder</td>
        <td>
          A file in <code>docs/</code> at a flat, lowercase URL:{" "}
          <code>/wiki/Getting-Started</code> becomes <code>/getting-started</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The page name above each page</td>
        <td>The page's <code>title</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>_Sidebar.md</code> groups</td>
        <td>
          <code>(group)/</code> folders with a <code>meta.ts</code>, which add a
          sidebar group but no URL segment
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Pages the sidebar doesn't list</td>
        <td>A collapsed "More pages" group</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>External links in the sidebar</td>
        <td>
          <a href="/docs/content/navigation#featured-links">Featured links</a>{" "}
          above the sidebar
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>[[Page Name]]</code> and <code>[[text|Page Name]]</code></td>
        <td><code>[text](/page-name)</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Links to <code>github.com/&lt;owner&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;/wiki/…</code></td>
        <td>Links to the new page</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>&gt; [!NOTE]</code> and the other alerts</td>
        <td><code>:::note</code> callouts, in <code>.mdx</code> pages</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Images stored in the wiki</td>
        <td>Files beside the pages, optimized by Blume</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>_Footer.md</code></td>
        <td>Footer links, or dropped</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Old wiki URLs</td>
        <td>A route table, and a link stub for each page to push to the wiki</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Here's one page before and after:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={beforePage} lang="md" title="Getting-Started.md (wiki)" />
  <CodeBlock
    code={afterPage}
    lang="mdx"
    title="docs/(start)/getting-started.mdx (Blume)"
  />
  <p>
    GitHub showed the page name above every page and each <code>#</code>{" "}
    heading below it, so a body H1 is a section. The H1 that repeated the page
    name is gone, and the others moved down a level. Most heading anchors
    keep their ids, since GitHub and Blume build them the same way.
  </p>
  <p>The sidebar becomes folders:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={sidebar} lang="md" title="_Sidebar.md (wiki)" />
  <CodeBlock code={tree} lang="txt" />
  <CodeBlock code={startMeta} lang="ts" title="docs/(start)/meta.ts" />

  <h2>Before you start</h2>
  <p>
    A wiki repository can't host the new site: GitHub runs no Actions in it
    and serves no Pages from it. Decide where the docs will live, usually a
    folder in your main repository or a repository of their own. The agent
    converts a copy of the wiki clone in place, and you move it there
    afterwards. Clone the wiki, copy it, and remove the copy's remote, so
    nothing in it can be pushed to the wiki by mistake:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={cloneWiki} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    GitHub publishes only the wiki's default branch, which is what the clone
    checks out. Then save the list of pages GitHub serves, from the wiki's own
    page index:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={listPages} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    Use it at the end to confirm that every page is accounted for. While
    you're in the repository settings, turn on{" "}
    <strong>Restrict editing to collaborators only</strong> under Features, so
    the wiki stops changing while you migrate it. You also need Node.js 22.19
    or later, and Claude Code or Codex installed and signed in.
  </p>

  <h2>Run the migration</h2>
  <p>From the copy, run:</p>
  <CodeBlock code="npx blume migrate github-wiki --claude" lang="bash" />
  <p>
    To use Codex, swap <code>--claude</code> for <code>--codex</code>. Name the
    source: a wiki has no config file, so Blume can't detect one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>blume migrate</code> converts nothing itself. It opens the agent on the{" "}
    <code>blume-migrate</code> skill inside the package, pointed at its GitHub
    wiki reference. Following it, the agent:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Asks where the docs will live, and converts the copy in place if you
      don't say.
    </li>
    <li>
      Runs the codemod's <code>routes</code> step, which lists the pages the
      way GitHub serves them and writes the route table,{" "}
      <code>wiki-routes.json</code>. It also flags the pages that only point
      elsewhere and the ones that aren't content.
    </li>
    <li>
      Classifies every page in the table: a page to keep, a moved notice
      mapped to its target, or a page to leave out (a mirror repository's
      README, a test page) set to <code>null</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Runs the codemod's <code>convert</code> step, which moves each page into
      place, writes its title, converts wiki links, alerts, headings, and
      images, and writes every <code>meta.ts</code> from{" "}
      <code>_Sidebar.md</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Works through what the codemod reports: links that were already broken
      on GitHub, uploaded images to download, MDX errors, image sizes, and the
      files the conversion left behind.
    </li>
    <li>
      Writes <code>blume.config.ts</code>, scaffolds <code>package.json</code>,
      and runs <code>blume build</code>, <code>blume validate --strict</code>,
      and the codemod's <code>check</code> until they pass.
    </li>
    <li>
      Prepares the link stubs for the old wiki and lists the links to it in
      your main repository.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Here's the <code>routes</code> step on the Acme wiki, and the table after
    classifying its one moved notice:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={routesOutput} lang="txt" />
  <CodeBlock code={routeTable} lang="json" title="wiki-routes.json" />
  <p>
    The agent ends with a summary of what it migrated, dropped, and
    approximated. Keep it for the review.
  </p>
  <Callout type="info">
    Two runs on one wiki can differ, since classifying pages and fixing what
    the codemod reports are judgment calls. Read the diff rather than trusting
    it.
  </Callout>

  <h2>What happens to the old wiki</h2>
  <p>
    Your old wiki URLs stay on github.com, and nothing can redirect them.
    GitHub has no redirect setting for wiki pages, and it strips scripts and
    meta refreshes from them. Blume's redirects can't help either, since they
    only match paths on your new site. Links in issues, pull requests,
    bookmarks, and search results keep pointing at the wiki. Here's what you
    can do instead, in this order.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Restrict editing</strong> to collaborators, if you haven't, so
      nobody edits pages that are about to move.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stop anything that syncs the wiki.</strong> Look for a workflow
      in your main repository that runs <code>on: gollum</code> (wiki edits),
      a mirror repository whose workflow pushes to the wiki, and a copy of that
      workflow inside the wiki itself. Left running, they revert your stubs or
      copy them into the mirror. The agent lists every one it finds.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Replace each page with a link stub</strong> once the new site is
      live. Each page keeps its name, so every old link still opens, now on a
      one-line page that links to the new one. The agent only prepares the
      stubs: it never commits or pushes to the wiki, so whether and when they
      go live is your decision. When you're ready, in a fresh clone of the
      wiki:
    </li>
  </ol>
  <CodeBlock code={pushStubs} lang="bash" />
  <CodeBlock code={stubPage} lang="md" title="Getting-Started.md (wiki)" />
  <p>
    The sidebar becomes a notice that the docs have moved, the footer goes, and
    images stay for sites that link them. Pages you left out keep their
    content, and the command lists them so you can decide. If you'd rather
    keep the old pages readable, add <code>--notice-only</code>: it only puts
    the notice at the top of <code>Home</code> and the sidebar.
  </p>
  <ol start={4}>
    <li>
      <strong>Update the links in your repository.</strong> Search your main
      repository for the wiki's URL:{" "}
      <code>git grep -n -i 'github.com/acme/widget/wiki'</code>. Issue templates,
      the README, CONTRIBUTING, and code comments often link it. The
      codemod's <code>check</code> prints each URL's new address and checks
      its anchor against your build, including anchors that were already
      broken on the wiki.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <CodeBlock code={checkInbound} lang="bash" />
  <CodeBlock code={checkInboundOutput} lang="txt" />
  <p>
    For a missing anchor it suggests the ids on that page with the same words,
    when there are some. Point the link at a real heading, or drop the anchor.
    Also set the repository's About website to the new site if it pointed at
    the wiki.
  </p>
  <ol start={5}>
    <li>
      <strong>Disable the wiki</strong> under Features in the repository
      settings, if you want, once your own links point at the new site. GitHub
      hides every page, stubs included, without deleting them, and turning the
      wiki back on restores them.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    To see whether search engines show your wiki, run{" "}
    <code>curl -sI https://github.com/acme/widget/wiki | grep -i x-robots-tag</code>.
    If it prints <code>x-robots-tag: none</code>, they don't. If it prints
    nothing, search results keep sending readers to the old pages for a while,
    and the stubs are what they find.
  </p>

  <h2>Review the changes</h2>
  <p>
    In the copy, run <code>git add -A</code>, then start with{" "}
    <code>git diff --cached --stat -M</code>, which pairs each page's old file
    with its new one. Run <code>npm run dev</code>, and work through these,
    which are where a wiki migration most often needs a second look.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The route table.</strong> Check each moved notice's target and
      each page set to <code>null</code>. A page that only links somewhere else
      without saying it moved is content, so the agent keeps it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Navigation.</strong> Click through the sidebar against the old
      one. A page listed twice keeps its first place. A page with pages nested
      under it in the sidebar is now a collapsible group, listed as its own
      first row. The "More pages" group holds everything the sidebar didn't
      list, sorted by title, so move pages out of it where they belong.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Links that were already broken.</strong> The codemod leaves a
      link to a page the wiki didn't have as it was and reports it. These
      often point at pages that moved to another repository, so retarget them
      or remove the link.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Images.</strong> Images uploaded through GitHub's editor are
      hosted outside the wiki. The agent downloads them next to the pages
      unless you object; for a private repository it has to, since they need a
      GitHub login. Widths and heights from raw <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tags
      are gone, and an image that pointed at nothing on GitHub is dropped.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Leftover files.</strong> Converting in place leaves the moved
      notices, the pages you left out, and images nothing uses where they
      were. They don't publish, but delete them so they don't confuse later
      edits: the wiki keeps them. Delete wiki tooling that no page needs any
      more too, like a sync script.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Leftover wiki links.</strong> Blume shows <code>[[Page]]</code>{" "}
      as text and doesn't warn about it. <code>grep -rn '\[\[' docs</code>{" "}
      should print only code samples.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The lockfile.</strong> A mirror repository's{" "}
      <code>.gitignore</code> often ignores <code>package-lock.json</code> or{" "}
      <code>yarn.lock</code>. Remove those lines so the lockfile is committed.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Check the route table</h2>
  <p>
    Build the site, then check that every page in the route table exists in
    the build, with its anchor where it has one. From the folder holding{" "}
    <code>blume.config.ts</code>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={buildAndCheck} lang="bash" />
  <CodeBlock code={checkTable} lang="txt" />
  <p>
    Then check every URL in the page list you saved, the same way:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={checkOldPages} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    Each old URL should come back <code>ok</code> with its new address,{" "}
    <code>moved</code> with a moved notice's outside target, or{" "}
    <code>DROPPED</code> for a page you left out on purpose, which counts as a
    problem so it stays visible. A <code>MISSING</code> line is a page the
    table doesn't have. Run <code>npx blume validate --strict</code> too, which
    checks every link and anchor on the new site.
  </p>

  <h2>Deploy</h2>
  <p>
    Many projects publish their docs on GitHub Pages, and a Blume site can go
    there.{" "}
    <a href="/guides/markdown-docs-github-pages">Deploy Markdown docs to GitHub Pages</a>{" "}
    has a complete workflow. Blume can't detect your URL there, so set it, with
    the repository name as the base for a project site:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`deployment: { site: "https://acme.github.io", base: "/widget" },`}
    lang="ts"
    title="blume.config.ts"
  />
  <p>
    On Vercel or Netlify, Blume detects the URL. For other hosts, see{" "}
    <a href="/docs/deployment">Deployment</a>. With a base, Blume prefixes it
    to Markdown links but not to raw HTML ones, and{" "}
    <code>blume validate</code> doesn't flag those, so add it by hand to any{" "}
    <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> the codemod reports, such as a wiki link it
    found inside an HTML block. Once the site is live, run the stubs command
    with its real address, push the stubs if you've decided to, and rewrite
    the links in your repository.
  </p>

  <h2>What doesn't carry over</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Editing in the browser.</strong> Anyone with access could edit a
      wiki page on GitHub. Changes now go through the repository. Set{" "}
      <code>github</code> in <code>blume.config.ts</code> once the docs live in
      one, and every page gets an <strong>Edit on GitHub</strong> link.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Page history.</strong> The revisions list, history, and compare
      views stay on the wiki. If you bring the wiki's history into your
      repository with <code>git subtree add</code>, <code>git log --follow</code>{" "}
      still finds it. If you turn on <code>lastModified: "git"</code>, though,
      every page's date starts at the migration, since every page was renamed
      and Blume's dates don't follow renames.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Redirects.</strong> Old wiki URLs never redirect, as above. The
      stubs are the closest you get.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wiki chrome.</strong> The Pages list, the clone box, footer text,
      sidebar prose, and links to headings in the sidebar.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Some rendering.</strong> Image sizes, GeoJSON maps and 3D
      models, and light and dark image pairs. Raw HTML that GitHub stripped,
      like inline styles and scripts, would now work, so the agent removes it or
      asks you about it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
  <h3>The build fails with an MDX error on a .mdx page</h3>
  <p>
    The page became <code>.mdx</code> for an alert, and it has raw HTML with
    Markdown inside, like a <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> block in a list. MDX
    reads Markdown inside HTML only with blank lines around it. Add them, or
    turn the block into Markdown.
  </p>
  <h3>validate reports a broken link to string;</h3>
  <p>
    A code block inside a quote, like this one, is misread as a link
    definition:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={quotedFence} lang="md" />
  <p>Move the code block out of the quote; the rest of the quote can stay.</p>
  <h3>validate reports an anchor that exists</h3>
  <p>
    Anchors are case-sensitive in Blume and weren't on GitHub. Match the case
    of the heading's id, like <code>#TsDeclFiles</code> for a link written{" "}
    <code>#TSDeclFiles</code>.
  </p>
</GuidePage>
